Windows/iOS ARM64EC: complete writes into trapped code pages that touch no code

On willfaust/FEX 18d891e + 0004 + 0005 (the build 49 pin). Not for FEX-Emu.

With a 64-bit Java process's code cache as plain memory ([x64-rwx]), every
write into a write-trapped 16 KB page invalidated every block compiled from
the page; FEX untrapped it, translated it all again and trapped it again.
HotSpot writes new code and data next to code it runs all the time: Project
Zomboid (Zulu 25, build 50) did about 658,000 translations and 176,000 SMC
faults before its main menu (4.5 minutes).

FEXCore/Utils/MadeiraSmcCodeMap.h keeps, per 16 KB page, which 64-byte
granules the decoder has read as code and whether FEX itself armed the
page's write trap. The decoder marks an instruction's bytes before it reads
them, then fences. A full-page invalidation (exclusive code-invalidation
lock) clears a page's bits; any protection change of a page clears its
armed flag; the trap sets it after its NtProtectVirtualMemory call.
The ARM64EC module hands the map to Madeira's ntdll through
NtQueryVirtualMemory class 1007. Madeira's fault handler
(build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c, virtual_ios.c [smc-emu]) completes an
ordinary or store-release store into an armed page of a plain [x64-rwx] view
through a second mapping of the page when the written granules hold no
code, then fences and checks again; a store that raced the decoder goes to
FEX's usual path, which runs it again.

Off by default (build 60: it tripled Project Zomboid's re-translations);
MADEIRA_SMC_WRITE_EMULATE=1 registers the map. Without it every write takes the
old path. The rebuilt module is app/Madeira/arm64ec-windows/xtajit64.dll,
built from 18d891e + 0004 + 0005 + 0006 with build/fex-arm64ec/build.sh's
options.

